course: dictionary
lesson: 7857
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence around the word tells you which one is meant. «Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold, and worn ones become rags or stuffing.» Here rags are old, worn-out clothes, because the sentence is talking about clothes that are too worn to sell.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "Worn ones become old, torn clothes or stuffing" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning. Ask yourself: what job is the word doing here?
explain: Another sentence: «Cai Lun improved papermaking about 1,900 years ago, using tree bark, old rags and fishing nets.» Paper has to be made from something, so here rags are old cloth used as material. The same word can name a thing, like a cloth, or a material you make things from.
explain: Rag can also be a verb. In UK informal English, «to rag someone» means to tease them. The words around it will show you which meaning the author means, every time.
fact: A rag is a piece of old cloth used for cleaning or wiping.
fact: Rags are old, torn clothes.
fact: «Rags to riches» means going from very poor to very rich, as in the story of Cinderella.
fact: «Like a red rag to a bull» means sure to make someone angry.
fact: In UK informal English, to rag someone means to tease them.
exercise: «Wipe the spill up with an old rag.» What is a rag in this sentence? | A bowl for soup | A kind of warm hat | A newspaper of poor quality | *A piece of old cloth for cleaning
exercise: Cinderella's story is often called «rags to riches». What does this phrase mean? | Wearing torn clothes to a ball | *Going from very poor to very rich | Being teased at school | Cleaning the house every day
exercise: Papermaking can use tree bark, old rags and fishing nets. What were the rags used for? | To keep people warm | To feed the animals | To make a fire | *To make paper
test: «He was like a red rag to a bull when they mentioned the match.» What does this phrase mean? | *Sure to make him angry | He liked the colour red | He was very tired | He wanted to run away
test: A UK friend says, «Stop ragging Sam, he's upset.» What does ragging mean here? | Shouting for help | Tearing paper | *Teasing | Cleaning
exam: «Clothes given to clothes banks are sorted: good ones are resold, and worn ones become rags or stuffing.» Which meaning fits rags best here? | *Old, torn clothes | A poor-quality newspaper | To tease someone | A type of paper
